    "transcript": [
      {
        "role": "H1",
        "text": "Bank Balance Sheets, Reserves, and Capital",
        "node": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "MACROECONOMICS",
        "node": 1
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "MACRO-20",
        "node": 2
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Time: 5 minutes",
        "node": 3
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Outcome: Classify bank accounts and trace how transactions change reserves and capital.",
        "node": 4
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Difficulty: Intermediate",
        "node": 5
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "THE CORE IDEA",
        "node": 6
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "A bank balance sheet satisfies assets = liabilities + capital. Reserves, loans, and securities are assets. Deposits and bank borrowing are liabilities. Bank capital is owners' equity: assets minus liabilities. Reserves provide liquidity and help meet withdrawals; capital absorbs asset losses. Required reserves and capital requirements therefore constrain different parts of the balance sheet.",
        "node": 7
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "HOW TO RECOGNIZE IT",
        "node": 8
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "•",
        "node": 11
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "A customer's deposit is a bank liability; the cash or reserve balance received is an asset.",
        "node": 12
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "•",
        "node": 14
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "A cash withdrawal reduces both reserves and deposits by the same amount.",
        "node": 15
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "•",
        "node": 17
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "A loan is a bank asset because the borrower owes the bank repayment.",
        "node": 18
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "•",
        "node": 20
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "An asset loss reduces capital when liabilities do not change.",
        "node": 21
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "WATCH OUT",
        "node": 22
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Do not call reserves bank capital. Reserves are assets available for payments; capital is the residual claim, assets minus liabilities, that cushions losses.",
        "node": 23
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "WORKED EXAMPLE: CAPITAL ABSORBS AN ASSET LOSS",
        "node": 24
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Before the loss, assets equal liabilities plus capital: $1,000 = $940 + $60. A $25 loan loss reduces loans and total assets while liabilities are unchanged. Afterward, $975 = $940 + $35. Capital absorbs the loss; reserves remain $120.",
        "node": 63
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "CHECK YOURSELF",
        "node": 64
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "If a depositor withdraws $20 in cash, which asset and which liability fall?",
        "node": 65
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "READY?",
        "node": 66
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Return to the game and master this concept.",
        "node": 67
      }
    ],
